7592186499448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499531}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Ratios by Rounding to the Nearest Hundred"} :step/id #uuid "64bb8dc3-c0c9-5342-9899-e92b00265817" :step/math-academy-id 648005 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499607}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499598 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499639 :tutorial/content "We can use what we know about rounding numbers to approximate ratios.\nWe round the quantities to a given degree first, before setting up the ratio.\n\nLook at the following table, which shows the number of girls in a ballet school with blonde or black hair.\n\n| Blonde Hair | Black Hair |\n| --- | --- |\n| $33$ | $58$ |\n\nRounding to the nearest ten, let's determine the approximate ratio of girls with blonde hair to girls with black hair.\n\nFirst, let's approximate the number of girls with blonde or black hair by rounding to the nearest ten.\n\n- Number of girls with blonde hair: $33 \\longrightarrow 30$\n\n- Number of girls with black hair: $58 \\longrightarrow 60$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of girls with blonde hair to the number of girls with black hair is approximately\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{60}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30 \\div 10}{60 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{3}{6}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 \\div 3}{6 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of girls with blonde hair to the number of girls with black hair is approximately $\\dfrac{1}{2}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "777fe794-e3cc-5250-a9e8-e407cc3f306c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2052 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "805b138d-9de3-5c18-96bd-507752cfcb3d" :step/math-academy-id 648000 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499610}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499634 :tutorial/content "Now, let's see how we can find a value given the ratio and another value.\n\nSuppose at a school library, there are some science books and $396$ history books. The ratio of the number of science books to the number of history books is $3: 4.$ By rounding the number of history books to the nearest ten, approximate the number of science books in the library.\n\nFirst, let's approximate the number of history books:\n\n$$\n396 \\longrightarrow 400\n$$\n\nThe ratio of the number of science books to the number of history books is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}4}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nHere, \n\n- the numerator represents the number of science books, and\n\n- the denominator represents the number of history books.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $400$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $100{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 \\times 100}{4 \\times 100} = \\dfrac{300}{400}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are approximately $300$ science books in the library." :tutorial/id #uuid "16e103a1-de00-5acb-9566-24168dec17eb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14923 :tutorial/title "Finding a Value Given a Ratio"} :step/id #uuid "9d54fdb2-7a7b-5fda-81dd-61e03fb3fa56" :step/math-academy-id 648008 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499610 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499435 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499520 :question/id #uuid "a8368c62-f936-54c5-b069-1adabcb11ffe" :question/math-academy-id "e-2281"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10523576-ab54-5731-bfaf-999a591295e0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499559}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Ratios by Rounding to the Nearest Ten"} :step/id #uuid "aa6626df-e1ca-57ff-8e3c-685699ee9c0c" :step/math-academy-id 648001 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499591}}] :activity/title "Approximating Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/iden